== Cryptography / ledger audit ==

[[Hash function]], [[Public-key cryptography]], and [[Digital signature]] remain general cryptographic concepts rather than blockchain inventions.

[[Blockchain]] is a particular distributed-ledger architecture using linked blocks and consensus; it is not declared equivalent to cryptocurrency or every distributed ledger.

[[Consensus mechanism]] is defined through agreement on valid distributed state, not through simplistic majority voting.

== Smart-contract / identity audit ==

[[Smart contract]] is executable replicated program/state and is not treated as a legal-contract synonym.

[[Cryptocurrency wallet]] manages cryptographic keys/addresses rather than literally containing ledger assets.

[[Decentralized identity]] remains broader than blockchain and broader than DID. W3C DID is used as a standards-backed implementation family.

[[Zero-knowledge proof]] preserves formal proof-system scope and rejects the common “zero knowledge = anonymous application” shortcut.

== DeFi / market audit ==

[[Decentralized finance]] is treated as an architecture family with measurable centralization dependencies, not as “finance without intermediaries” by definition.

[[Automated market maker]] remains a broad family. [[Constant-product market maker]] is one mechanism; [[Concentrated liquidity]] is one liquidity-allocation design.

[[Liquidity pool]] and [[Liquidity provider]] remain inventory/state and participant-role concepts respectively.

[[Arbitrage]], [[Slippage]], and [[Price impact]] preserve general market meanings. In particular:

<pre>
price impact
= execution-price movement caused by the trade itself

slippage
= difference between expected/quoted and realized execution,
  potentially including price impact plus intervening market movement and other effects
</pre>

== Oracle / attestation audit ==

[[Oracle (blockchain)]] imports external data; it is not declared a source of truth.

[[Attestation]] records an assertion with provenance/integrity evidence; cryptographic validity does not establish factual truth of the claim.

This distinction preserves earlier [[Evidence]], [[Provenance]], [[Verification]], and [[Identity]] semantics.

== Cross-wave audit ==

Wave 11 connects to Wave 10 without redefining it:

* consensus mechanisms can contain incentive/game-theoretic structure but are not game theory itself;
* decentralized governance uses [[Governance]] but blockchain voting is not the definition of governance;
* Sybil resistance is relevant to consensus, reputation, identity and governance without making [[Sybil attack]] a blockchain-only concept;
* DeFi mechanisms instantiate incentives and strategic interaction without defining [[Incentive]], [[Game theory]], or [[Collective action]].

Wave 11 also preserves Waves 6–9 boundaries around cryptography, distributed systems, information, identity, verification, robustness, and agents.

